# Cooperation between covalent organic frameworks (COFs) and metal organic frameworks (MOFs): application of COFs-MOFs hybrids

Rui Xue1 · Hao Guo2 · Wu Yang2 · Sheng‑Li Huang1 $\cdot^{\cdot}$ Guo‑Yu Yang1
Abstract
The application and development of porous materials have a long history. With the deepening of study, more and more advanced organic porous materials and organic–inorganic porous materials have been designed and prepared. Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) materials, as typical representatives among them, have attracted extensive attention all over the world since the first report, and show great application potential in the fields of adsorption, catalysis, drug delivery, luminescence, sensor, separation analysis, energy storage, and others. COFs-MOFs hybrid materials combine the advantages of COFs and MOFs, and are gradually being reported in the fields of sensors, energy storage, adsorption, catalysis, and biological applications. The current research results show that the COFs-MOFs hybrid materials have ingenious obvious structural characteristics and superior performance, and will be widely applied in more and more fields with the deepening of research and development. It is believed that the cooperation between these two famous materials will bring more surprises to the world.
Keywords  Covalent organic frameworks $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ COFs $\cdot\cdot$ Metal organic frameworks $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ MOFs $\cdot\cdot$ Hybrid materials
## 1  Introduction
Porous materials have been used for a long time in history of human. In the past few decades, researches on porous materials have grown rapidly due to their wide application in sensing [1, 2], gas storage [3, 4], catalysis [5, 6], energy storage [7, 8], adsorption [9, 10], and many other fields [11]. Porous materials are including mineral clay [12, 13], silicate [14, 15], metal oxides [16, 17], film materials [18], porous metals [19, 20], carbon materials [21, 22], organic polymers [23, 24], and inorganic/organic polymer materials [25–27]. As shown in Fig. 1, organic porous materials can be divided into organic porous materials, inorganic porous materials, and inorganic/organic porous materials according to their composition.
Porous materials were developed rapidly with the continuous progress of experimental technology; more and more porous materials were designed and synthesized successfully. The most reported porous materials mainly fall into the following categories: (1) natural minerals, such as zeolite and haloite, (2) synthetic zeolite, such as molecular sieve, (3) ordered mesoporous materials, such as MCM−41 and MCM−48, (4) metal–organic coordination materials, such as MOFs and ZIFs, (5) organic porous materials composed of light elements (C, H, O, N, B, etc.) through rigid covalent bonds, such as PAFs and COFs. In recent years, more and more researches of porous materials constructed from organic units have been taken, and a lot of progress has been achieved.
Organic porous materials formed from light elements (e.g., H, C, N, O, B) connected by rigid covalent bonds further enrich the variety of porous materials, so they present better stability, lower density, and higher specific surface area. This material possesses both the properties of porous material and organic polymer, has received widespread attention once it has been reported. In addition, a variety of organic porous materials can be constructed through different modification methods. Compared with inorganic porous materials, organic porous polymer materials have more diversified structures, simple synthesis methods, and structural designability, thereby the application fields were expanded widely.
In recent years, with the research of the organic porous materials developed, several types of organic porous polymer materials were reported, such as hyper-crosslinked polymers (HCPs), polymers of intrinsic microporosit (PIMs), conjugated microporous polymers (CMPs), porous aromatic frameworks (PAFs), and covalent organic frameworks (COFs). Otherwise, inorganic/organic porous materials have also been studied extensively and made significant progress, especially metal organic frameworks (MOFs). Both MOFs and COFs were first reported by prof. Yaghi and coworkers in 1995 and 2005 respectively [28, 29]. In recent 5 years (2017–2021), more than 5700 papers about COFs and 50,000 papers about MOFs have been published(from Web of Science), which show the importance of these two famous materials (Fig. 2). MOFs and COFs present remarkable research potential in many fields including catalysis, gas storage, optoelectricity, fluorescence, dye and pollution adsorption, drug delivery, separation analysis, electrochemical sensor, and power storage devices [30, 31] due to their extraordinary properties in excellent thermal/chemical stability, outstanding porosity, and flexible designability. With the deepening of study on MOFs and COFs, better performance, stronger practicality, and wider application areas have been developed via combining MOFs (or COFs) with other materials, such as functional carbon materials [32], conducting polymers [33], Mxenes [34], metal oxide [35], and metal sulfide [36]. In recent researches, two brilliant materials, MOFs and COFs, were employed simultaneously to obtain a fascinating functional composite material. COFs-MOFs hybrid materials show wonderful application performance in many fields, such as gas storage and separation, catalysis, membrane separation, pollutant removal and adsorption, and chemical sensor.
In this review, the applications of MOFs, COFs, and COFs-MOFs hybrid materials are summarized. The challenges and potential applications of the COFs-MOFs compound are discussed. Finally, some personal views on the future research direction of cooperation between MOFs and COFs are put forward in order to stimulate more exciting research in the future.
## 2  Brief introduction of metal organic frameworks
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), are obtained by linking inorganic (metallic) and organic units by coordinate bonds [37, 38]. Tens of thousands of MOFs were constructed and reported in the last 20 years, illustrating a high research enthusiasm of the chemist all over the world. The explosive growth in the preparation, characterization, and application of MOFs was attributed to designability and tunability of structures and pores. The structure of MOFs can be designed based on spatial configurations of the coordination of metal ions or metal ion clusters with organic ligands according to the target properties. Nowadays, MOFs have emerged as a kind of fascinating materials for various applications in the fields of gas adsorption/separation [39, 40], heterocatalysis [41–43], photocatalysis [44–46], magnetism [47], drug delivery [48, 49], pollutant adsorption [50], luminescence [51–53], electrochemical sensor [54], separation analysis [55], energy storage [56–59], and others [60, 61]. Only one example of each application can be listed due to space limitations.
As is known to all, separation or purification of gas is important and difficult problem in petrochemical industry, such as separation/purification for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{n}/C H_{4}}$ . To solve this problem, a novel MOF $(\mathrm{[Ni(dpip)]}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ with a mixed N, O-donor ligand (4,6-di(pyridin-4-yl) isophthalic acid was designed and synthesized successfully by Li and coworkers in 2021 [39]. What’s really interesting is that there are two types of one dimensional channels with different sizes in this prepared MOF. The MOF presents remarkable adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . Furthermore, excellent dynamic separation ability for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ , and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C H_{4}}$ was obtained.
As of 2017, Mn-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 with spherical structure and petal-like shape respectively were prepared successfully by Jiang et al. via hydrothermal method [41]. Both Mn-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 showed good adsorption and activation abilities for NO and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . The experimental results suggested that both Brønsted and Lewis acid sites exist in Mn-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74. NO and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ could further react to form $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under the action of the catalyst (Mn-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74).
Energy and environment are severe problems that restrict the development of all countries in the world. More and more attention has been attracted to hydrogen lately as a new potential green source for power generation due to its high energy content and non-pollution. MOFs can be a new kind of photocatalysts for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Ni-BDC (nickel-benzene dicarboxylic acid) and Ni/Cu-BTC (nickel/ copper-benzene tri-carboxylic acid) that respectively have mono and binary central metal ions were synthesized and reported by Asmaa S. Morshedy and coworkers. These two MOFs presented a good photocatalytic performance for hydrogen production through the water-splitting process [44].
Three $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ -based MOFs were constructed by $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ ions and dipyridyl ligands, namely, $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}{-}D P)C l]_{n}}$ , $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-DP})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cll}_{\mathrm{n}}\right.$ , and $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}{-}T M D P){-}C l]_{n}}$ , respectively [47]. M. Naqi Ahamad and coworkers found that $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}–D P)C l]_{n}}$ displayed fluorescence quenching toward all nitroaromatic compounds (NACs), and $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}-}$ $\mathrm{DP})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cl}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ can be used as a selective and sensitive sensor to detect m-DNB. Interestingly, the magnetic studies showed that both $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}–D P)C l]_{n}}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-DP})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cll}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ exhibit strong ferromagnetic and $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu(4,4^{\prime}-T M D P)-C l]_{n}}$ presents moderate antiferromagnetism.
MOFs exhibit extraordinary adsorption performance due to huge specific surface area and well‐developed pore structure. Among the reported MOFs, many isostructural MOF74 s were particularly known due to the excellent adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [62]. Based on high surface area and active metal sites, MOF-74 is considered to be a promising candidate as drug carrier [48] or contaminant adsorbent [50]. Hu and coworkers reported well-shaped Mg-MOF-74 nanorods with different sizes obtained by controlling the crystallization process via two steps solvothermal method. The prepared Mg-MOF-74 nanorods presented a high-capacity drug carrier with $\upalpha$ -cyano-4-hydroxycinnamate (CHC) loading of $62.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ . As an excellent adsorbent, MOFs also showed high performance in pollutant removal. Sun and coworkers prepared a new nanostructured Fe–Co-based MOF$74(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1}\mathrm{MOF}{-}74),$ adsorbent successfully through a simple solvothermal method [50]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ MOF-74 showed a good adsorption ability of arsenic (As(III) and As(V)) in aqueous solution. The maximum adsorption capacities of As (III) and $\mathrm{As(V)}$ in aqueous solution were 266.52 and 292.29 $\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ , respectively, which indicated a potential application of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ MOF-74 for removal of arsenic.
In recent years, MOFs have been increasingly used in analytical sciences, such as fluorescence detection [51], electrochemical sensing [54], and separation analysis [55], due to the typical structure and properties. Guo and coworkers reported a new pillar-like 3D lanthanide-organic framework (Eu-MOF) constructed by $^{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylic acid and $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ ions through in situ hydrothermal reactions [51]. The prepared Eu-MOF showed strong luminescence and high structural stability in aqueous or organic solvents. Eu-MOF presented the potential application of selective and sensitive luminescent probe to rapidly detect $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ and nitrobenzene. In 1999, a famous open-framework metal coordination polymer $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{3}(T M A)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}]_{n}}$ was synthesized by Chui and coworkers, termed as HKUST-1 [63]. Ma and coworkers developed a novel electrochemical sensor based on HKUST-1 [54]. The composite of HKUST-1- and electroreduction graphene oxide–modified electrode was prepared and applied as a new electrochemical sensor for simultaneous determination of dopamine and paracetamol with excellent sensitivity, selectivity, and stability. Furthermore, the large surface area and particular adsorption property allow MOFs to be an expectant separation media for chromatography. Xu and coworkers developed an in situ layer-by-layer self-assembly method to combine HKUST-1 on the inside of the capillary column for open tubular capillary electrochromatography (OT-CEC) [55]. A series of capillary columns with different nano-HKUST-1 layers were successfully prepared. The separating performances of the prepared open tubular (OT) capillary columns were investigated with some neutral small organic molecules. The experimental results illustrated that four neutral small organic molecules (benzene, naphthalene, acenaphthene, phenanthrene) and five phenolic compounds ( $m$ -dihydroxybenzene, $o$ -nitrophenol, $o$ -dihydroxybenzene, $m$ -nitrophenol, $p$ -nitrophenol) were separated successfully with HKUST-1-modified capillary columns due to the excellent size selectivity of pore structure and the hydrophobicity of organic units in HKUST-1. The present work indicated the advantages and potential of MOFs in analytical science.
It is worth mentioning that the MOF-based energy storage materials have attracted more and more attention due to high energy density and excellent stability. Especially, MOFs are important to improve the energy storage performances for supercapacitors. As reported by Sun and coworkers, three amino-functionalized bimetal MOFs (NiCoMOFs) with different morphologies, such as nanospheres, nanosheetassembled hollow spheres, and rhombus sheets, were synthesized successfully [56]. The NiCo-MOF with nanosheetassembled hollow spheres showed excellent electrochemical properties with the specific capacitance of $1126.7\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the cycle retention of $93\%$ . The asymmetric supercapacitor device based on NiCo-MOF and activated carbon present the energy density of $20.94~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at power density of $750.84~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ .
Yaghi et al. summarized the common ligands of MOFs and the classic MOFs [64], Stock and Biswas studied the synthetic methods of MOFs [37] in their published reviews, respectively. The structure and properties of MOFs can be tuned by changing metal elements and related ligands, which is one of the reasons for the rapid development of MOFs. In order to expand the application of MOFs and improve its performance, MOFs are usually combined with other materials. This review mainly focuses on the combined utilization of MOFs and COFs reported already.
## 3  Brief introduction of covalent organic frameworks
Since the first COF was reported [29], more and more research papers and reviews about the preparation, characterization, and application of COFs were published, which amply demonstrates the superiority of this material. COFs were constructed by linking organic units through strong covalent bonds [65]. Unlike other organic polymers, COFs are a class of crystalline porous organic polymers (POPs) which exhibit significant feature such as atomically precise incorporation, extensible structures, ordered nanopores, designability of the structure, controllable synthesis, and manageable function [66]. COFs are composed of light elements (C, N, O, B, P, S, Si, H) through covalent bonds, such as B–O, C–N, B–N, N–N, $-C=\Nu-$ , $-\Nu=\Nu-$ , and B–Si–O. As reported, COFs are usually constructed by polymerization reactions, for instance, the dehydration reaction of boronic acid [29, 67], dehydration of boronic acid and silanol [68], polymerization of boronic acid and catechol [69], Schiff base reaction of aldehyde and amine [70, 71], condensation of aldehydes and hydrazides [72], cyclotrimerization of nitrile [73], dehydration condensation reaction of amino and acyl chloride [74], condensation of squaric acid with amines [75, 76], condensation of amino and anhydride [77, 78], and so on. Most frequently reported synthetic methods for COFs are solvothermal [79, 80] and ionothermal [81, 82]. Microwave-assisting method can enhance synthesis efficiency sometimes [83, 84]. In addition, some other synthesis methods were also reported, such as solvent-free mechanochemical synthesis method [85], vapor-assisted conversion [86], solid–vapor interface reaction [87], and synthesis under ambient conditions [88]. COFs have shown great potential for application by mixing the brilliant properties and the outstanding advantages, for example, light quality, low density, large specific surface area, porosity, extraordinary stability, and designability of the structure. Many excellent properties and applications have been studied, for instance, gas storage and separation [89, 90], membrane separation [91], heterocatalysis [92], photocatalysis [93], fluorescence detection [94], electroanalysis [95], separation and analysis [96], drug delivery [97], pollutant removal [98], solar cell [99], energy storage [100, 101], and so on. A brief introduction for these applications was given in this review.
As Yaghi’s group reported in 2010 [89], COF-10 shows a good storage performance for ammonia with the highest capacity of $15\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . COF-10 was prepared by condensation of BPDA (biphenyldiboronic acid) and HHTP (hexahydroxytriphenylene) with B-O linkages [102]. COF-10 has a huge surface with a large number of boron sites. The boron sites provide an active adsorbent surface for interaction with the Lewis basic gas (e.g., ammonia). In addition, gases can be separated by changing the structure and surface properties of COFs. Li and coworkers synthesized a new 2D azinelinked COF (ACOF-1) by condensation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ and 1,3,5-triformylbenzene successfully [103]. ACOF-1 with huge surface area $(1000\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ showed a good storage performance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\Nu_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . A mixed-matrix membrane (MMM) based on Matrimid and ACOF-1 was developed by Shan and coworkers [90]. The MMMs showed a good separation performance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture. COF membrane can be utilized not only for gas separation, but also for the separation of dye molecules. Hao and coworkers prepared COF-TpPa1-modified membrane by chemical vapor deposition successfully [91]. The prepared COF-TpPa1 membrane exhibited outstanding operation stability and excellent water permeance with high rejection for different dyes and low rejection for salts.
COFs were selected as the shell material in heterogeneous catalyst by Li and coworkers [92]. $({\mathrm{Pd}}/{\mathrm{C}})\ @$ TpPa COFs with yolk-shell structure were designed to catalyze the Suzuki reaction. This material exhibited a high conversion with a low Pd loading of the reaction between phenylboronic acid and bromobenzene. Furthermore, $({\mathrm{Pd}}/{\mathrm{C}})\ @ $ TpPa COFs presented an excellent stability compared to bare $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ , and also showed a good size cutoff efficiency.
Yang and coworkers synthesized an olefin-linked 2D-COF(TTO-COF) with triazine groups via Aldol condensation reaction of TFPT(2,4,6-tris(4-formyl-phenyl) -1,3,5-triazine) and TMT(2,4,6-trimethyl-1,3,5-triazine) [93]. TTO-COF showed an excellent photoresponsive semiconductor performance. Organic dyes (MO and MB) can be photocatalytic degraded by TTO-COF; furthermore, trifluoromethylation reactions can be photocatalyzed too.
The first luminescent COF (TP-COF) consisted of 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) and pyrene-2,7- diboronic acid (PDBA) by Jiang and coworkers in 2008 [104]. The unique structure and designability allowed a great potential of COFs in fluorescence detection. A new corrole-based COF (CorMeO-COF) was synthesized via imine condensation reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ TPAPC(5,10,15-tris(p-aminophenyl)corrole) and DMCHO(2,5-dimethoxy-terephthalaldehyde) by Li and coworkers [94]. Strong fluorescence quenching of CorMeO-COF caused by photo-induced electron transfer (PET) process allowed to detect $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sensitively and selectively. Furthermore, some trivalent metal ions (Ga(III), Al(III), $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ , and $\mathrm{Fe(III)},$ ) can be selectively detected by fluorescence enhancement of CorMeO-COF. In recent years, COFs have also been employed to develop electrochemical sensors. Guan and coworkers prepared a new COF through Schiff base reaction of DP(2,6-diaminopyridine) and TFPPy(1,3,6,8- tetra(4-formyl-phenyl) pyrene), named DP-Py COF [95]. A new electrochemical sensor for selective and sensitive determination of caffeine (CAF) and theophylline (TP) was fabricated by compounding DP-Py COF with AuNPs. DP-Py COF/AuNPs/GCE electrode showed sensitive voltammetric response and wide linear range to CAF and TP; the detection limits were calculated as $0.076~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $0.19~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ respectively. In addition to chemical sensors, COFs are also used in separation analysis, such as chromatography. LZU-1 was designed and synthesized by Wang and coworkers for the first time, and was applied in catalysis [105]. A COFs-silica composite $(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{rLZU}1_{\cdot}$ was prepared via in situ growth strategy by $\mathrm{{Xu}}$ and coworkers [96]. LZU1 grew in situ on the surface of porous silica spheres to obtain stationary phase $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{rLZU}1$ . The $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{rLZU}1$ presented large specific surface area, good stability, and excellent porous structure, which distinctly improved the capacity factor. Chromatographic column based on $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{rLZU}1$ showed good resolution and reproducibility in chromatographic separations of acidic, basic, and neutral compounds. Furthermore, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@$ rLZU1 can also be applied in separation of the real samples, presented the potential application in chromatographic separation analysis.
Anbazhagan and coworkers prepared a thioetherterminated triazole bridge–containing COF (TCOF) [97]. The prepared TCOF-PEG carrier showed good biocompatibility toward HeLa and cells. As the author reported, the TCOF system provided a new method for biomedical field, and may be useful in many biomedical applications. In addition to being excellent adsorbents for gases and drugs, COFs are also good candidate for adsorbing pollutants, such as mercury. A sulfhydryl functionalized COF was reported as an efficient adsorbent for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ by Cao and coworkers [98]. COF-SH showed a good adsorption performance due to the effective adsorption site for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ capture based on electrostatic attraction or chelation effect of the sulfhydryl group on the COF-SH.
As potential materials for photovoltaic applications, 2D COFs have emerged as potential solar cell materials due to rigid plane structure and strong interactions with light. Kuo and coworkers synthesized two 2D COFs by the condensation of 4, $4^{\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime\prime}.$ -(ethane-1,1,2,2-tetrayl)tetranilino (ETTA) and $_{3,3^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}}$ -tetraformyl- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -bicarbazole (Car-4CHO) and 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(4-formyl-phenyl)pyrene (TFPPy), respectively (Car-ETTA and TFPPy-ETTA) [99]. The power conversion efficiency (PCE) can be enhanced up to $19.80\%$ by the COF-modified perovskite solar cells (PVSCs). In addition to solar cells, COFs are also more and more used in energy storage, such as supercapacitors [100] and ion batteries [101]. A layered 2D Aza-COFs(Hex-Aza-COFs) were designed and synthesized successfully that utilized as the negative electrode material for pseudocapacitors [100]. The capacitance at $1~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of Hex-Aza-COF-1, Hex-AzaCOF-2, and Hex-Aza-COF-3 was $220\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , $585\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , and $585\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ respectively. An asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) solid-state device was prepared by employing Hex-Aza-COF-3 as negative electrode material and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ as positive electrode material respectively. The potential window and capacitance of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}//\mathrm{Hex}$ -Aza-COF-3 ASC solid-state device are 1.7 V and $64~\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ $(1~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The energy density of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}//$ Hex-Aza-COF-3 is $23.3~\mathrm{W\cdoth\cdotkg^{-1}}$ at power density of 661.2 $\mathbf{W}{\cdot}\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Aza-COF with phenazine-decorated channels was synthesized via a condensation reaction of hexaketocyclohexane (HKH) and 1,2,4,5-benzenetetramine (TAB) by Shehab and coworkers [101]. The average specific capacity, energy density, and power density of the Aza-COF-based electrode were calculated as $550\ \mathrm{mA\cdoth{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , $492~\mathrm{W\cdoth\cdotkg^{-1}}$ (at $0.1\mathrm{C})$ , and $1182\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ (at $40\mathrm{C}$ respectively.
Nowadays, more and more COFs with new structures and properties were synthesized; the structural types and application fields of COFs were further expanded, indicating the great potential of this material. In addition, COFs can sometimes be used in combination with other materials to obtain more interesting properties.
## 4  Cooperation between COFs and MOFs
MOFs and COFs have similarities and differences in structure and function. Combining the two advanced functional materials can give play to their common advantages and improve their shortcomings, thereby showing fascinating properties. Recently, COFs and MOFs were used in combination to obtain better performance. At present, COF-MOF composite materials are mainly applied in fields of sensors, energy storage, adsorption, catalysis, etc. The applications of COFs-MOFs composite materials will be introduced in this section.
## 4.1  Sensors
A COF(MCA) based on melamine and cyanutic acid was synthesized, and combined with Ce-MOF to prepare
Ce-MOF $@$ MCA [106]. The as-synthesized Ce-MOF@MCA materials presented many outstanding features, such as good stability, excellent dispersion ability, high electrochemical activity, and strong bio-affinity due to the superior features of the MCA and Ce-MOF. Ce-MOF $@$ MCA hybrid material was employed as a new OTC aptasensor with an extremely low LOD of 35.0 fM. Furthermore, Ce-MOF@MCA aptasensor showed excellent reproducibility, accuracy, and applicable in the analysis of real samples. Another electrochemical aptasensor based on COF-MOF hybrid was established by Liu and coworkers [107]. The prepared $\mathbf{Co-MOF}@$ TPN-COF-based aptasensor toward ampicillin presented very high sensitivity $(\mathrm{LOD}=0.217~\mathrm{fg{\cdotmL^{-1}})}$ , excellent reproducibility, good stability and acceptable applicability due to rich nitrogen-related functional groups (triazine rings) in TPN-COF, efficient synergy between TPN-COF and Co-MOF, and brilliant bio-affinity between aptamer strands and the Co-MOF $@$ TPN-COF hybrids. Zhang and coworkers developed an electrochemical analytical method for detecting adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and chloramphenicol (CAP) based on $\operatorname{MOF@COF}$ core–shell hybrid materials [108]. The UiO-66 $\mathbf{-NH}_{2}@\mathbf{COF}$ -based aptasensor was fabricated by the utilization of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as a core and TAPB-DMTP-COF as a shell. The aptasensor exhibited an outstanding sensing performance for ATP detection with a wide range of $1.0\times10^{-5}$ to ${5.0\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ , and a low limit of detection ${5.04~\mathrm{fg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ .
The composite material of UIO-66 and COFs can be applied in electrochemical sensors as well as fluorescence sensors. Wang and coworkers explored the optical properties and fluorescence analysis applications [109] of $\mathrm{UiO}@\mathrm{COFs}$ UiO-66 and the COFs were selected based on high affinity, multi-emission, structural selectivity, and good stability. The prepared $\operatorname{MOF@COF}$ composite showed an excellent performance of ratiometric fluorescence sensing and visible detection of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ and ATP.
Both MOFs and COFs exhibit extremely high performance in analytical applications, such as high sensitivity, low detection limit, and wide linear range. The exploration of COFs-MOFs hybrid materials is important because of their unique properties, but the reports about the applications of COFs-MOFs hybrid in electrochemical biosensing, especially in food safety testing, are still rare.
## 4.2  Energy storage
Energy storage is an important field where COFs and MOFs play a role. Moreover, COFs-MOFs hybrid materials exhibit good performance in the field of energy storage too. Samoì and coworkers prepared aza-MOF $@$ COF by azaDiels–Alder cycloaddition unprecedentedly [110] (Fig. 3a). The aza-MOF $@$ COF exhibited high specific capacitance of $20.35\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , extraordinary stack capacitance of 1.16, and good cycling stability with capacitance retention of $89.3\%$ after 2000 GCD cycles.
Recently, Sun and coworkers reported a coordinationinduced interlinked hybrid of imine-based COFs and Mnbased MOFs (COF/Mn-MOF) [111] (Fig. 3b). The COF/ Mn-MOF exhibited a larger capacity of $1015\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ after 650 cycles due to the involvement of $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ centers and C $=\mathbf{C}$ of benzene rings for lithium storage. Moreover, the derived $\mathbf{MnS}@\mathbf{NS-C}$ composites also showed an excellent lithium storage performance.
The porous frameworks such as MOFs and COFs can supply the huge specific surface area and the pores with controllable size and effective charge transport. Both of them can play their own structural advantages in the hybrid material, thus showing excellent energy storage performance.
## 4.3  Heterogeneous catalysis
Gao and coworkers prepared a new core–shell COF/MOF hybrid by growing the TpPa-1 on the surface of PCN222-Co [112] (Fig. 4a). The prepared PCN-222-Co $@$ TpPa-1 showed a good thermal and chemical stability, remarkable recyclability, and high catalytic activity. PCN-222-Co $@$ TpPa-1 was used to catalyze deacetalization-Knoevenagel reaction based on the cooperation between Lewis acid sites and Brønsted base active sites in PCN-222-Co and TpPa-1, respectively.
A sandwiched MOF $@$ COF composite which can selectively catalyze hydrogenation according to the size of the reactants was successfully prepared by Zhou and coworkers [113] (Fig. 4b). Pd-loaded $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}{\bf H}_{2}$ was functionalized with terephthalaldehyde, and then reacted with 1,3,5-tris(4- amidophenyl)triazine to form $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}@C O P}$ ; the $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}@C O P}$ was treated with the 2,5-dihydroxy1,4-benzenedicarboxaldehyde to obtain $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH}_{2}@$ COF finally. The prepared sandwiched $\mathrm{Pd/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ COF composite exhibited a high catalytic activity for liquidphase hydrogenation of three olefins (cyclohexene, transstilbene, and triphenylethylene) and showed a good size selectivity.
Cai and coworkers prepared a COFs/MOFs compound with core–shell structure by using $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe) as core and NTU-COF as shell, which was connected by covalent bonds [114] (Fig.  4c). Since the hydrophilic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe) nanocrystals tend to aggregate the hydrophobic reactant (styrene) and promote the radical mechanism path after binding to the hydrophobic NTU-COF shells, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{NTU}$ composites show significantly enhanced catalytic conversion and selectivity in the process of styrene oxidation.
In heterogeneous catalyzed reactions, MOFs can provide stable structural support, as well as a large number of active sites. COF shells can be used as a size-selective regulator, and composite materials with different surface properties can be obtained by changing the molecular structure. In addition, the good stability of MOF-COF-based hybrids can provide support sites for other catalytic substances (such as Pd, Pt nano particles) and expand its catalytic applications. It is expected that the MOF-COF-based hybrids will reveal more and more opportunities for other heterogeneous catalysts.
## 4.4  Photocatalysis
The applications of COFs-MOFs hybrid materials in photocatalysis currently mainly include photocatalytic hydrogen production, photocatalytic chemical reactions, and photocatalytic degradation.
Chen and coworkers constructed a core–shell $\mathbf{MOF}@$ COF hetero-framework via in situ growth of a hydrazonelinked COF(TFPT-DETH) on the surface of a $\mathbf{MOF}(\mathbf{NH}_{2}-$ UiO-66) [115]. These two interesting materials were selected to construct the hetero-framework due to good stability of TFPT-DETH and regular morphology and adaptability with TFPT-DETH of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. The as-prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@$ TFPT-DETH hetero-framework exhibited a good hydrogen evolution rate of $7178\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\cdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}}$ , which showed a better performance than the reported COF-based photocatalysts. This advantage is attributed to the synergy of TFPT-DETH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, such as expanded absorption of light, preferable dissolution and transfer of excitons, and unique pore structures. By combining $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 with another COF, Zhang et al. also obtained a COFs-MOFs hybrid material with photocatalytic hydrogen production capability [116]. A new type of MOFs-COFs hybrid porous materials ­( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66/TpPa-1-COF) with huge surface area and high crystallinity was prepared via covalently anchoring $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 onto the surface of TpPa-1-COF.
The effective viable-light absorption of $\mathrm{TpPa-1-COF}$ , wellmatching band gaps between TpPa-1-COF and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, and efficient charge separation across the covalent heterojunction interface in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66/TpPa-1-COF hybrids allowed a superior photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of 23.41 m mol· $\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ . Li and coworkers fabricated hierarchical porous MOFs-COFs hybrid materials by postsynthetic covalent modification [117]. Benzoic acid–modified covalent triazine-based frameworks (B-CTF-1) were covalently bonded with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ respectively to construct two MOFs-COFs hybrid materials. $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/B-CTF-1 showed high hydrogen production rate of $360\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under visible light.
Lu et al. coated the COFs ­ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL- $125\textcircled{a}$ TAPB-PDA) on the MOFs $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{MIL}\mathrm{-}125)$ to form ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}-125@$ TAPB-PDA hybrids by a seed growth method [118]. The prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL- $125@$ TAPB-PDA nanocomposites exhibited efficient and stable photocatalytic activity for selective oxidation of alcohols under visible light. In another paper, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 was chosen to build a sandwich MOFs $@$ COFs hybrid due to its great performance in catalysis [119]. Di-methylated COF-LZU1(DM-LZU1) was coated on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125, and the Pt nanoparticles were encapsulated between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 and DM-LZU1 to form sandwich structure. The $\mathrm{Pt}~\mathrm{NPs}$ promoted the charge separation on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125, and the hydrophobic porous DM-LZU1 fully concentrated the reactants, and pores ensured the rapid electron and mass transfer between Pt NPs and reactants, so Ti$\mathbf{MOF@Pt@DM-LZU1}$ showed high photocatalytic activity for hydrogenation of olefins with hydrogen under visible light. Sun and coworkers prepared a core–shell $\mathbf{MOFs}@$ COFs hybrid by combination of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 and LZU1 ether, and the prepared hybrids also showed excellent photocatalytic performance [120].
COFs-MOFs hybrids also show good application potential in the degradation and treatment of pollutants. Zhao and coworkers prepared a core–shell hybrid (IR-MOF3 $@$ COFLZU1) based on a Zn-MOF and COF-LZU1. IR-MOF3 $@$ COF-LZU1 exhibited a high activity of photocatalytic degradation of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{p}}$ -nitrophenol [121]. He and coworkers constructed a COF-MOF hybrid by encapsulating $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) with TTB-TTA. The prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/TTB-TTA showed a good performance of photodegradation of methyl orange [122]. UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{MA}$ and M $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{IL}-101-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ $\mathrm{TpMA}$ were prepared by Lv and coworkers [123]. Both two composites showed good photodegradation abilities of bisphenol A under visible light. In addition, the hybrid of MOF-based materials and COFs was reported to be used for the photocatalytic reduction of heavy metal ion, such as $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , as Xue and coworkers reported [124].
Compared with MOFs and COFs, COFs-MOFs-based hybrid materials still remain largely uninvestigated. The combination modes between MOFs and COFs are Van der Waals forces or hydrogen bonds, which leads to poor stability of the MOFs-COFs hybrid materials. Therefore, a new strategy to prepare novel COFs-OFs hybrid materials with high stability and excellent physiochemical properties should be explored. The combination between MOFs and COFs by chemically covalent bonds can not only improve the stability of the hybrids, but also can enhance the photocatlaytic activity by effective separation of photo-induced charges.
The core–shell type structure can integrate the functionalities of core and shell, forming a more regular spatial arrangement and oriented charge transfer path, and that encourages better charge transfer. Furthermore, the absorbance wavelength, charge lifetime, and quantum yields would be improved by assembling into a core–shell type hybrid material [115]. The present work illustrated that COFs-MOFs hybrid systems have great potential in the field of photocatalysis.
## 4.5  Gas storage and separation
The storage and separation of gas are of great significance to the treatment of polluting gases ­ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , etc.), the storage of combustible gas $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , etc.), and the proper storage of industrial gas (CO, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , etc.). Porous materials can play a huge role in this field. As mentioned above, there have been many reports on the application of COFs and MOFs in the field of gas storage and separation, so the application of their composite materials in this field has attracted attention gradually. Luis and coworkers encapsulated UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in COF-TAPB-BTCA via a two-step method of spray-drying and amorphous-to-crystalline transformation [125]. The UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@$ COF-TAPBBTCA presented a significant increase of both $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake. The authors suggested this material can be applied in water-sorption applications and fabrication of COFsMOFs membranes. Saikat and Ben prepared a composite membranes through growing COF-300 on UiO-66 membrane [126]. The [COF-300]-[UiO-66] composite membrane exhibited an excellent separation ability with high selectivity. Fu and coworkers also prepared a COF/MOF hybrids based on COF-300 [127]. They grew the MOF $\left(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})\right)$ on the COF (COF-300) to prepare a hybrid polymer membrane for the first time. The prepared [COF-300]- $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ membrane presented better separation selectivity of $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ mixtures than the COFs or MOFs membranes. As reported by Cheng et al., $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas mixture was separated selectively by UiO$66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{Pa}{-}1$ hybrid [128]. In addition, the UiO$66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{Pa}{-}1$ hybrid showed a good stability.
## 4.6  Adsorption and removal of pollutants
With the development of industry, energy crisis and environmental pollution are attracting more and more attention. In addition to degrading the pollutants with suitable conditions, some interesting materials can also be used to adsorb and remove the pollutants. COFs, MOFs, and other porous materials have been studied a lot; recently, the derived COFs-MOFs hybrid materials are gradually applied to curb environmental pollution based on the excellent adsorption performance. Zhong and coworkers prepared a binary nanocomposite of COFs-MOFs ­ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TpPa}\mathrm{-}1\big>$ by a solvothermal method [129] (Fig. 5a). $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@$ TpPa-1 hybrid presented a good performance of adsorption and photocatalysis for radionuclides removal. $\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{-MIL}\mathbf{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@$ $\mathrm{TpPa}{\cdot}1$ had a high adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}(536.73\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Eu^{3+}(593.97~m g~g^{-1})}$ ascribed to the chelation and electrostatic attraction of nitrogen/oxygen-containing functional groups. Karmakar and Li designed and prepared a MOP(metal–organic polyhedra)-COFs composite that exhibited brilliant performance for the capture of metal- and metalloid-based oxoanions such as $\mathrm{HAsO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-},\mathrm{MnO}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ [130] (Fig. 5b). Firoozi and coworkers described a novel COFs-MOFs hybrid material prepared by hybridizing Zn-MOF-5 and melamine-terephthaldehydebased COF. The COFs-MOFs hybrid material presented a brilliant adsorption performance for adsorption of AO and RB dyes due to the strong covalent bonds between MOFs and COFs [131] (Fig. 5c).
Pollutants are diverse and complex, some are harmful, and some can be recycled. COFs-MOFs hybrid materials can sieve and selectively enrich different pollutants through precise structural design, which can improve the removal efficiency of pollutants and save costs. Furthermore, the removal and separation efficiency of pollutants can be increased by changing the surface properties and magnetic properties of the material. In addition, the strategy of contamination remediation can be developed by hybridizing with materials with photocatalytic properties; thus, the pollutants can be degraded or converted into clean substances.
## 4.7  Biological applications
Compared with the other applications, COFs-MOFs hybrid composites applied in biology and medicine are rare. In 2017, Zheng and coworkers synthesized a nanoscale MOF $@$ POP composite (UNM) by epitaxial growth of porphyrin-POPs on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. The prepared UNM can generate ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ under various experimental conditions, which showed the potential application in photodynamic therapy (PDT) efficacy [132]. Dong et al. reported a method to protect ultrathin two-dimensional porous organic nanosheets (PONs) by encapsulating PONs with ZIF-8. The composites can be used for in vitro bioluminescence imaging of live cell due to highly stable, porous, fluorescent of the prepared composites [133]. Li and coworkers developed sacrificial template method to prepare capsules for enzyme encapsulation by using MOFs as sacrificial templates to construct biomacromolecule $@$ MOF $@$ COF core–shell structures (Fig. 6). The composites can provide the encapsulated biomacromolecules against external perturbations. The encapsulated enzymes under the protection of COF capsules presented brilliant reusability than the other traditional porous materials [134].
The COFs-MOFs hybrid materials can play important role as high-performance COF bioreactors, cell imaging agent, drug delivery device, and the mimetic enzyme with biocatalytic function in the cell. However, the construction and characterization of its fine structure, the improvement of surface hydrophilic properties, and the reduction of preparation costs are problems need to be resolved.
## 5  Prospect and conclusions
COFs and MOFs materials have attracted extensive attention all over the world since the first report, and show great application potential in the fields of gas adsorption and separation, catalysis, drug delivery, pollutant adsorption, luminescence, electrochemical sensor, separation analysis, energy storage, and others. Their application areas will expand with the gradual deepening of research. Both COFs and MOFs have peculiar structures and various properties, and the combination of the two is the inevitable result of their development. Excitingly, composite materials based on these two materials also show significative application potential in many fields.
So far, there are two main strategies for the preparation of COFs-MOFs hybrid materials, which are MOFs-mediated hybrid materials (MOFs $@$ COFs) and COFs-mediated hybrid materials $(\mathrm{COFs}@\mathrm{MOFs})$ [135]. Adding COFs or MOFs materials to the core–shell reaction system is currently a relatively successful preparation method. The better suitability of COFs and MOFs, the better structure and properties of their composites, is well reflected by their performance in the field of catalysis applications. Therefore, selecting and designing a rational space structure and reactive monomers with specific functional groups is an effective way to improve their structure and performance.
There are still some difficulties in the preparation of COFs-MOFs hybrid materials, and the control of reaction conditions is very important for their synthesis; otherwise, it is difficult to obtain regular molecular structure and morphology. Second, the structural design should match its application; otherwise, its structural advantages will not be fully played. Characterization is also important for COFsMOFs hybrid materials, and according to the current literatures, IR, XRD, SEM, TEM, TGA, XPS, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR, and $\Nu_{2}$ absorption and desorption tests have been almost indispensable for the characterization of such materials; in addition, STM is increasingly reported for the characterization of COF materials. With the development of characterization techniques, we can obtain a more adequate observation of this material. Furthermore, scientific characterization and theoretical simulation should be combined, which is very helpful and useful for understanding and designing the materials.
In addition, COFs and MOFs in the hybrids should not be mixed together simply, but should focus on the matching of their structures and functions, so that they can combine their advantages to achieve the effect of $^{\cdot}1+1$ is greater than 2.” This is another difficult point in the development of COFsMOFs hybrid materials, but also one of the most important problems to be solved. Therefore, the design of such materials should be based on the target function. The COFs (or MOFs) with appropriate spatial structure and physical/ chemical properties should be constructed by selecting the monomers with appropriate functions and sizes, and the monomers should be connected by appropriate linkages. The befitting connection will enable COFs and MOFs to cooperate fully and perform excellent functions.
In conclusion, porous materials have been widely applied and continuously developed, and more porous materials are designed and synthesized. This review summarizes the applications of COFs-MOFs composites that have been reported so far based on the current status of the applications of COFs and MOFs materials. This paper can help people to understand the current status of the application of such materials, which is especially important for those who are just starting out.
